1
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Heaven is falling, so Earth must move

2
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Asteroid vs Earth is a adventure, action, science
fiction film directed by Christopher Ray.

3
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:51,000
When a shower of massive meteors threatens an extinction level on Earth, the world's greatest minds
devise a dangerous plan that will take the planet off its axis in order to avoid the impact.

4
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Marissa Knox, played by Tia Carrere.

5
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:21,000
General Masterson, played by Robert Davi.

6
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Captain Rogers, played by Tim Russ.

7
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:51,000
Lt. Commander Chase Seward, played by Jason Brooks.

8
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:06,000
Chief of the Boat, played by Darin Cooper.

9
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:21,000
Lt. Rouse, played by Robert R. Shafer.

10
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:36,000
Asteroid impact avoidance encompasses the methods by which near-Earth objects (NEO) on a potential
collision course with Earth could be diverted, preventing destructive impact events.

11
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:51,000
An impact by a sufficiently large asteroid or other NEOs would cause, depending on its impact location, massive tsunamis or multiple firestorms, and an
impact winter caused by the sunlight-blocking effect of large quantities of pulverized rock dust and other debris placed into the stratosphere.

12
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:06,000
A collision 66 million years ago between the Earth and an object approximately 10 kilometers (6 miles) wide is thought to have produced the Chicxulub crater and
triggered the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event that the scientific community understands to have caused the extinction of all non-avian dinosaurs.

13
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:21,000
While the chances of a major collision are low in the near term, it is a near-certainty
that one will happen eventually unless defensive measures are taken.

14
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
Astronomical events—such as the Shoemaker-Levy 9 impacts on Jupiter and the 2013 Chelyabinsk meteor, along with the growing number of
near-Earth objects discovered and catalogued on the Sentry Risk Table—have drawn renewed attention to such threats.

15
00:48:45,000 --> 00:48:51,000
The popularity of the 2021 movie Don't Look Up helped to raise
awareness of the possibility of avoiding NEOs.

16
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
Awareness of the threat has grown rapidly during the past few decades, but much more needs to be accomplished before
the human population can feel adequately protected from a potentially catastrophic asteroid impact.

17
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:21,000
In 2016, a NASA scientist warned that the Earth is unprepared for such an event.

18
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
In April 2018, the B612 Foundation reported "It's 100 percent certain we'll be hit by a devastating asteroid, but we're not 100 percent sure when." Also in 2018,
physicist Stephen Hawking, in his final book, Brief Answers to the Big Questions, considered an asteroid collision to be the biggest threat to the planet.

19
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:51,000
Several ways of avoiding an asteroid impact have been described.

20
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
There are two primary ways: to modify the trajectory of the object so that it does not collide with the Earth, or to modify the object by breaking it up
so that the resulting fragments do not collide with the Earth or their
smaller size reduces the subsequent hazard posed to the Earth.

21
01:08:15,000 --> 01:08:21,000
Nonetheless, in March 2019, scientists reported that asteroids may be
much more difficult to destroy than thought earlier.

22
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
An asteroid may reassemble itself due to gravity after being disrupted.

23
01:14:45,000 --> 01:14:51,000
In May 2021, NASA astronomers reported that 5 to 10 years of preparation may be needed to avoid a virtual
impactor based on a simulated exercise conducted by the 2021 Planetary Defense Conference.

24
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
In 2022, NASA spacecraft DART impacted Dimorphos, reducing the
minor-planet moon's orbital period by 32 minutes.

25
01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:21,000
This mission constitutes the first successful attempt at asteroid deflection.

26
01:24:30,000 --> 01:24:36,000
In 2027, China plans to launch a deflection mission to the near-Earth object
2015 XF261, with the impact estimated to occur in April 2029.

27
01:27:45,000 --> 01:27:51,000
The tone is thought_provoking.
